WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.200
This is the story of a journey to the Moon…

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.600
…and of the rocks we brought back.

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.800
After liftoff, three astronauts traveled for about four days to reach the Moon.

4
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.400
Michael Collins would remain in orbit aboard the Columbia module,

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.600
while Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin would descend to the surface in the lunar module Eagle.

6
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.200
On July 20, 1969, the Eagle separated from the Columbia

7
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.000
and fired its descent engine for over twelve minutes.

8
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.200
Below lay the Sea of Tranquility—a vast, dark plain.

9
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.800
“Houston, Tranquility Base here. The Eagle has landed.”

10
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.600
Hours later, Neil Armstrong descended the ladder and stepped onto the Moon.

11
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Nineteen minutes later, Buzz Aldrin followed him.

12
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.400
“That's one small step for a man, one giant leap for mankind.”

13
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.800
One of their first tasks was to collect an ‘emergency’ sample,

14
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.000
in case they had to leave in a hurry.

15
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.400
Before planting the flag or taking photos,

16
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.000
the priority was to secure a piece of the Moon.

17
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.400
For about two hours, they walked around, took photographs, and collected rocks and soil.

18
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.000
They ventured up to 100 meters away from the module

19
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.400
and gathered 21.55 kilograms of lunar material.

20
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.800
They also left behind scientific instruments:

21
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.800
a seismometer to detect moonquakes,

22
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.800
and a retroreflector—an array of mirrors

23
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.000
used to measure the distance to Earth via laser.

24
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.400
That mirror is still in operation today.

25
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.800
After 21 and a half hours on the Moon, the Eagle took off,

26
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.600
rendezvoused with Collins, and began the journey home.

27
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.600
On July 24, the Columbia capsule splashed down in the Pacific Ocean.

28
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.600
The rocks had arrived home.

29
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.400
In the laboratory, the rocks revealed a secret.

30
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.600
They were basalt—lava that cooled about 3.6 billion years ago.

31
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.600
Those tiny holes are gas bubbles

32
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.800
that were trapped while the lava was still hot.

33
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.800
The dark seas we see from Earth are ancient lava.

34
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.000
The Moon, so tranquil today, once had a fiery past.

35
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.200
Chapter I · Rock & Roll. The rock tells the story.

36
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.800
Now, it's your turn to investigate these rocks.
